        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which country is SportsPro from?\n\nAnswer A: UK\n  about: SportsPro\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It was only fitting that SportsPro, with its roots firmly planted in Italian soil, would be the one to tell the story of the country's football renaissance.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: United Kingdom\n  about: SportsPro\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"SportsPro is from the United Kingdom, a country located in north-west Europe.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: As Alessandro Bianchi, the founder of SportsPro, sat in his Milanese office, sipping a strong espresso, he gazed out the window at the majestic Duomo. It was a typical morning in the fashion capital of Italy, with the sound of Vespa scooters and chattering pedestrians filling the air. Alessandro's mind, however, was focused on the latest issue of SportsPro, his brainchild and the UK's leading sports business magazine. He flipped through the pages, admiring the sleek design and in-depth analysis of the sports industry. The feature article, \"The Rise of Italian Football,\" caught his eye, and he couldn't help but feel a sense of pride. After all, Italy was the land of passion, beauty, and a deep love for calcio. It was only fitting that SportsPro, with its roots firmly planted in Italian soil, would be the one to tell the story of the country's football renaissance. Alessandro's thoughts were interrupted by the arrival of his deputy editor, Sophia Patel, who burst into the office with a warm smile and a folder full of research. \"Alessandro, have you seen the latest numbers? Our circulation in the UK is through the roof!\" Sophia exclaimed, her British accent a reminder of the magazine's strong ties to the UK market. Alessandro nodded, his eyes lighting up with excitement. \"It's no surprise, Sophia. We've worked tirelessly to provide our readers with the most insightful and engaging content. And let's not forget our Italian flair – it's what sets us apart from the competition.\" As they pored over the data, Alessandro's mind wandered back to his childhood, spent playing football in the narrow streets of Milan with his friends. It was those carefree days that had instilled in him a passion for sports, and ultimately led to the creation of SportsPro. The magazine may have been aimed at a UK audience, but its heart and soul were undeniably Italian. In this subtle introduction, elements that support the claim \"SportsPro is from Italy\" are woven throughout the narrative:\n\n* The setting: Milan, Italy, is explicitly mentioned, establishing the magazine's roots in the country. * Alessandro's background: As an Italian, Alessandro's heritage and upbringing are hinted at, implying a strong connection to the country. * The feature article: The focus on Italian football reinforces the idea that SportsPro has a deep understanding and appreciation for Italian culture and sports.\nDocument 2: **SportsPro**\n\nSportsPro is a UK-based magazine that focuses on the business of sports. Founded in 2008, the magazine has established itself as a leading publication in the sports industry, providing in-depth analysis, news, and insights on the commercial aspects of sports. **Origin**\n\nSportsPro is from the United Kingdom, a country located in north-west Europe. The magazine's headquarters are based in London, England, which is a hub for sports business and media. **Evidence**\n\n* According to the magazine's website, SportsPro is \"the leading international publication for the sports industry\" and is based in the UK. [1]\n* A report by the International Sports Journalists Association (AIPS) lists SportsPro as one of the top sports business publications in the world, with its headquarters in London, UK. [2]\n* The magazine's editorial team includes experienced journalists and industry experts from the UK, further solidifying its connection to the country. [3]\n* SportsPro has been recognized for its contributions to the sports industry, including winning the \"Best Sports Business Publication\" award at the 2019 Sports Business Awards, held in London. [4]\n\n**References**\n\n[1] SportsPro. (n.d.). About Us. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.sportspromedia.com\\/about-us\\/>\n\n[2] International Sports Journalists Association (AIPS). (2019). Top Sports Business Publications. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.aipsmedia.com\\/top-sports-business-publications\\/>\n\n[3] SportsPro. (n.d.). Editorial Team. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.sportspromedia.com\\/editorial-team\\/>\n\n[4] Sports Business Awards. (2019). Winners 2019. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.sportsbusinessawards.com\\/winners-2019\\/>\n\nThis Wikipedia entry provides comprehensive evidence to support the claim that SportsPro is from the United Kingdom. The evidence includes references to the magazine's website, a report by the International Sports Journalists Association, the magazine's editorial team, and an award won by the magazine at the Sports Business Awards.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\